  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. With no noise, steps settle for learning rate < 0.5, swing back and forth without settling at 0.5, and grow above 0.5.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise per component has SD 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)): 2 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at batch 64, centred on the true slope.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at true step length (scale 1): orange = the next actual mini-batch step, grey = −learning rate × the true slope. A single noisy step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 8, the loss at the bowl's x edge (diverged). The ball and paths are capped at the bowl edge on screen, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next noise values. The ball takes 5 steps a second at 1× speed."
  ]
}